## Official edition declaration

Paracine 2027 defines a statically typed, ownership-aware, effect-checked native programming language and a complete, verification-centered compiler architecture. Its contract connects source text, types, lifetimes, authority, execution, optimization and native artifacts through an explicitly defined semantic chain. Its canonical compilation path proceeds from strict Unicode source validation through SAGE analysis, verified PCIR, PMIR, native x86-64 instruction encoding, COFF object construction, integrated linking and PE32+ binary validation.

The edition consolidates the 68 retained Edition-2026 constitutional resolutions and the Edition-2027 normative resolution register NLR-01–NLR-77. It replaces separate supplementary narratives with one coordinated language standard. Each feature belongs to its relevant semantic, runtime, analysis or target chapter; repeated feature names resolve to one typed contract.

The governing production disciplines are **defined behavior, enforced authority, correct ownership, precise effects, legal optimization, native ABI integrity and reproducible evidence**. Mandatory properties are checked; unsupported facilities are rejected; target security requirements fail closed. Optional heuristics never become proof.

> **Industrial principle:** Understand the intended computation. Establish its meaning. Navigate its dependencies. Preserve every governing obligation. Select a legal native realization. Verify its result.

## Scope and terminology

**MUST**, **MUST NOT**, **SHALL**, **SHALL NOT**, **SHOULD** and **MAY** are normative requirement terms. A *qualified implementation* satisfies its applicable published conformance profile. A *semantic fact* is established by language rules and verified judgments. A *candidate*, *estimate* or *conjecture* is not a semantic fact. A *target contract* determines explicitly target-dependent behavior without rewriting target-independent language rules.

All example source fragments use four-space indentation and logical newlines, without C++-style statement terminators. References to compiler/runtime operations define their contractual role; the canonical API and ABI signatures govern exact invocation.


## Binding interpretation and reconciled decisions

The definitions in this standard are cumulative and edition-aware. The following resolutions establish one binding interpretation wherever earlier language descriptions used overlapping terminology.

| Area | Binding Edition-2027 interpretation |
|---|---|
| Grammar punctuation | The grammar uses `::=` and `NEWLINE`/`INDENT`/`DEDENT`. Grammar notation never imposes source semicolons. |
| Reserved versus contextual words | Structural introducers are reserved only where the lexical profile states; most advanced facility names remain ordinary typed symbols or contextual words. |
| `give` and `return` | Both perform the same ordinary routine return operation in Edition 2027, including ownership transfer, inferred successful result construction and cleanup. Edition 2026 retains its original syntax. |
| `fail` and `raise` | Both construct or propagate recoverable typed error outcomes within compatible result contexts. They do not perform terminal exception unwinding. |
| `try`, `attempt`, `except`, `rescue`, `recover` | `try` propagates typed error results; `attempt` matches recoverable outcomes; `except` matches a typed error; `rescue` handles remaining recoverable errors; `recover` handles failures after checkpoint restoration. Terminal traps bypass them all. |
| `halt` and `trap` | Both are terminal, process-wide and non-unwinding. `halt` carries an explicit status. Ordinary `return` and graceful exit retain their separate cleanup contracts. |
| `maybe<T>` versus `unknown` | `maybe<T>` is an exact alias of `option<T>`; `unknown` is an explicit information state in `knowledge<T>` and never an implicit Boolean value. |
| `seq` versus Sequence | `seq` identifies Sequester only in its explicitly qualified operation context; `sequence` names ordered progression. They have distinct semantic identities. |
| `sign`, `resign` and re-signing | `sign(value)` classifies numeric sign; `crypto.sign`/`crypto.verify` handle signatures; `resign` relinquishes a role; `crypto.re_sign` renews signatures. |
| `size` type and `size(...)` operation | `size` is the target-width integer type in type position. `size(value)` is a typed operation that reports the container's documented logical extent; `size_of<T>()` reports target layout. |
| `invert`, `inverse`, `negate` | Inversion is an explicitly defined involution or orientation change; inverse is a mathematical/domain operation that can fail; negate is logical or additive negation. |
| `release`, `delete`, `empty`, `depop` | Release ends a resource lifecycle; delete removes an eligible owned/stored entity; empty removes all collection elements; depop removes selected elements. Destruction and ownership stay explicit. |
| Snapshot, checkpoint and continuation | Snapshot captures immutable eligible state; checkpoint journals reversible changes; rollback restores and exits; revert restores and continues; resume consumes a valid suspended execution continuation. |
| Memory and deep Capsule isolation | `memory` uses approved target-enforced separation; `deep` uses isolated execution plus authenticated, mediated calls. `var self` across a process boundary is remote exclusive invocation, never an exported mutable pointer. |
| `trust` and `unsafe` | Trust is evidence or a scoped assumption, not authorization to bypass checks. Unsafe authority allows specified operations with explicit invariants and cannot remove mandatory security boundaries. |
| Smart division and `/` | Integral `/` retains defined modular edge handling and terminal division-by-zero traps. `smart_div` is checked/policy-driven. Reciprocal rewrites require numerical equivalence under the selected domain. |
| Posit format | `posit<N,2>` uses the applicable Posit Standard 2022 profile; other valid `ES` values are Paracine-defined extended posit formats with explicit conversion and rounding semantics. |
| Float and decimal exceptional conditions | Decimal uses IEEE-style defined exceptional values and status observation; strict arithmetic preserves signed zero, rounding, NaN/NaR and required exception flags. `@fastmath` authorizes only explicitly selected relaxations. |
| `respond` and Chain `give` | `respond` is for typed protocol handlers. Ordinary Chain stage outputs use `give`; Chain dependencies and parallel policies remain explicit. |
| `spawn` and `postpone` | `spawn` creates scoped native threaded work. `postpone` submits owned work to an explicit scheduler. Neither introduces implicit `async/await`. |
| `@derive` and inline macros | `@derive` generates compiler-defined typed implementations. Edition-2027 inline macros are bounded, typed and hygienic; general text preprocessing and arbitrary user compiler code remain excluded. |
| `include` and `exclude` | These select language symbols or data. They do not load raw source files as a textual preprocessor. |
| `store`, `bank`, `deposit`, `withdraw` | Store selects an authorized storage domain. Bank accounts for eligible owned resources or typed quantities. Deposit and withdraw are typed transfers governed by conservation and synchronization. |
| `bypass` and `ignore` | Bypass selects a permitted optional route; ignore intentionally discards a permitted value. Neither suppresses mandatory safety, terminal failure, security or effect obligations. |
| Smart paths and hot swaps | Hot/warm/cold *paths* are workload classifications. Hot/warm/cold *swaps* are different runtime component-replacement contracts; a profile classification does not itself authorize code replacement. |
| FFI and syscall | FFI invokes explicit target-ABI foreign functions. Syscall access requires a target-specific service contract and raw authority where applicable; Windows kernel service numbers are never presumed stable. |
| ABI and diagnostics | PDB information supports debugging, not semantic proof; COMDAT/ICF must preserve observable identity; CFG is enabled only with valid complete guard data. Warnings cannot suppress mandatory errors. |
| Target qualification | Windows x86-64 with Microsoft x64 ABI, COFF and PE32+ is the canonical target. ELF, Mach-O and additional ABIs follow separate target profiles without altering target-independent semantics. |
| Evidence | Language legality, reference-IR verification, native execution tests, binary security qualification and benchmark measurements remain distinct evidence classes. No report substitutes an estimate for a measurement. |

**Precedence.** Frozen Edition-2026 constitutional resolutions apply to Edition-2026 source; Edition-2027 amendments govern Edition-2027 source; target profiles govern only target-dependent behavior; standard-library contracts govern library operations; informative examples never override normative text. An invalid or unsupported operation is rejected with a stable diagnostic rather than assigned invented behavior.


