# PART IX — SEMANTIC INTELLIGENCE, KNOWLEDGE AND RULES

## 83. Sage Foundational Semantic Authority


SAGE is the compiler's comprehensive semantic analysis and guidance system.

It maintains three permanently distinguished responsibilities.

### 6.1 Authoritative Semantic Analysis

This establishes whether a program satisfies the language.

It resolves:

- Names and declarations.
- Types and generics.
- Capabilities.
- Overloads.
- Ownership and borrows.
- Effects.
- Contracts.
- Arithmetic.
- Lifetime and provenance.
- Resource rules.
- Security boundaries.
- ABI obligations.

Its results form the authoritative Semantic Knowledge Model.

### 6.2 Structural Analysis

Structural Analysis identifies computational organization.

It recognizes loops, pipelines, state machines, dataflow, dependency graphs, resource patterns, closure compositions and architectural boundaries.

Structural classifications inform diagnostics, optimization and POLR.

They do not override semantic facts.

### 6.3 Stylometric and Adaptive Analysis

Stylometry examines project-local coding and computational patterns.

It supports diagnostic prioritization, naming consistency, optimization-search ordering and developer guidance.

Adaptive analysis evaluates candidate effectiveness and compilation cost.

Neither establishes type correctness, ownership legality or security.

The permanent hierarchy is:

**Semantic authority > structural classification > conceptual, stylometric and adaptive guidance.**

