## APPENDIX F — EXPRESSION AND DECLARATION GRAMMAR DETAILS


NLR-01 introduced the principal concrete syntax. To make its grammar usable as a parser contract, the following subordinate productions is incorporated into the grammar annex.

```

QualifiedName   = Identifier,
                  { ".", Identifier | "::", Identifier } ;

GenericParams   = "<", GenericParam,
                  { ",", GenericParam }, ">" ;

GenericParam    = Identifier, [ ":", Type ]
                  | "const", Identifier, ":", Type ;

Type            = [ "own" | "ref" | "mut" | "raw" ],
                  TypePrimary ;

TypePrimary     = QualifiedName, [ TypeArguments ]
                  | "(", Type, { ",", Type }, ")"
                  | "[", Type, ";", ConstExpression, "]" ;

TypeArguments   = "<", TypeArgument,
                  { ",", TypeArgument }, ">" ;

TypeArgument    = Type | ConstExpression ;

Field           = Attributes, [ Visibility ],
                  [ "let" | "var" ],
                  Identifier, ":", Type,
                  [ "=", Expression ], NL ;

VariantCase     = Identifier,
                  [ "(", Type, { ",", Type }, ")" ], NL ;

AliasDecl       = "alias", Identifier,
                  [ GenericParams ], "=", Type, NL ;

ConstantDecl    = "const", Identifier, ":", Type,
                  "=", ConstExpression, NL ;

StaticDecl      = "static", [ "var" ],
                  Identifier, ":", Type,
                  [ "=", ConstExpression ], NL ;

Expression      = Conditional ;

Conditional     = Pipeline,
                  [ "if", Expression,
                    "else", Conditional ] ;

Pipeline        = OrExpr, { "|>", OrExpr } ;
OrExpr          = AndExpr,
                  { ("or" | "||"), AndExpr } ;

AndExpr         = Equality,
                  { ("and" | "&&"), Equality } ;

Equality        = Comparison,
                  [ ("==" | "!="), Comparison ] ;

Comparison      = RangeExpr,
                  [ CompareOp, RangeExpr ] ;

CompareOp       = "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
                  | "is" | "in" ;

RangeExpr       = BitOrExpr,
                  [ (".." | "..="), BitOrExpr ] ;

BitOrExpr       = BitXorExpr,
                  { "|", BitXorExpr } ;

BitXorExpr      = BitAndExpr,
                  { "^", BitAndExpr } ;

BitAndExpr      = ShiftExpr,
                  { "&", ShiftExpr } ;

ShiftExpr       = AddExpr,
                  { ("<<" | ">>"), AddExpr } ;

AddExpr         = MulExpr,
                  { ("+" | "-"), MulExpr } ;

MulExpr         = Unary,
                  { ("*" | "/" | "%"), Unary } ;

Unary           = ("+" | "-" | "!" | "not"
                  | "~" | "move" | "try"), Unary
                  | Postfix ;

Postfix         = Primary,
                  { CallSuffix | IndexSuffix
                  | MemberSuffix } ;

CallSuffix      = "(", [ Arguments ], ")" ;
IndexSuffix     = "[", Expression, "]" ;

MemberSuffix    = ".", Identifier
                  | "::", Identifier ;

Arguments       = Argument, { ",", Argument } ;

Argument        = [ Identifier, ":" ], Expression ;

Primary         = Literal
                  | QualifiedName
                  | "self"
                  | "(", Expression, ")"
                  | ArrayLiteral
                  | ConstructorExpression
                  | ClosureExpression
                  | AskExpression ;

AskExpression   = "ask", Postfix,
                  "with", QualifiedName,
                  "(", [ Arguments ], ")" ;

ConstExpression = Expression ;

Identifier      = UnicodeXIDIdentifier
                  - ReservedKeyword ;

```

Patterns, capabilities, contracts, policy clauses, closures, foreign signatures and literal formats use their edition-specific syntactic categories; unsupported token sequences are rejected rather than parsed by guesswork.

Those rules cannot be responsibly considered settled merely by naming their constructs. A final parser-ready specification should publish them in a single machine-readable grammar file and include parser conflict tests.

In particular, the following rules must be reconciled with the productions above:

- Numeric and string-literal grammar.
- Generic type arguments versus constant arguments.
- Qualified-name and member-access ambiguity.
- Callable-type grammar.
- Closure-capture syntax.
- Array and record construction.
- `choose` patterns and exhaustiveness.
- Statement-only versus expression forms.
- Foreign variadics and `...`.
- Named return-borrow origins.
- Source attribute placement.
- Trailing comma and newline rules.

The binding implementation contract should forbid a parser from accepting unspecified forms simply because its parser library happens to recognize them.


