# 19. HOW EXPLOITABLE IS PARACINE?

Paracine is designed to make common low-level vulnerability classes difficult to introduce through ordinary safe code.

Its memory and ownership rules prevent invalid safe-language access patterns.

Bounds validation protects indexed operations.

Definite initialization prevents uninitialized safe values.

Its concurrency model governs conflicting mutable access.

Defined arithmetic avoids ambiguity associated with unspecified integer overflow behavior.

Capsule authority restricts access to protected objects and operations.

Deep isolation establishes a stronger operating-system enforcement boundary around designated components.

### Security exposure and governing controls

| Security area | Governing protection |
|---|---|
| Memory corruption in safe code | Ownership, borrowing, provenance and bounds |
| Resource misuse | Controlled lifecycle and release |
| Data races | Structured concurrency and synchronization |
| Unauthorized Capsule access | Capability enforcement and isolation |
| Invalid remote handles | Identity and generation validation |
| Malformed protocol data | Typed decoding, bounds and schema validation |
| Unauthorized native access | Explicit unsafe and Host authority |
| Invalid dynamic replacement | Qualified Patch and Swap validation |
| Untrusted dependencies | Package identity, hashes and trust policy |
| Incorrect numerical transformations | Semantic optimization constraints |

Paracine retains deliberate low-level capabilities through unsafe code, FFI, raw pointers, memory-mapped interfaces and native system operations.

These operations remain security-sensitive because they interact with resources and behaviors outside the safe-language contract.

Application-level security also remains important. Correct memory access does not independently establish correct authorization, confidentiality or business logic.

Paracine's security design is therefore layered: language safety governs ordinary computation, explicit authority governs privileged operations, and qualified isolation protects selected execution boundaries.

**Its central security strength is that unsafe authority, protected memory, runtime isolation and semantic correctness have distinct, enforceable responsibilities.**

